## 1. Inverse procedural material and texture modeling (11 October 2026)

### 1.1 The skeleton shared by every inverse procedural method (11 October 2026)

- Representation: a directed acyclic graph of typed operators (generators, filters, blends) whose parameters are continuous values, integers and random seeds; Substance Designer graphs in most papers, Blender shader graphs in VLMaterial, and a hand written lattice model in the oldest work. (11 October 2026)
- Forward evaluator: cooking the graph into maps, sometimes followed by a differentiable renderer that turns base color, normal and roughness into an image under known lighting. (11 October 2026)
- Comparison: a loss mixing deep texture statistics (Gram matrices of VGG features), spectra, a very low resolution pixel term, and sometimes learned perceptual similarities (LPIPS, DreamSim, CLIP). (11 October 2026)
- Search: retrieval of a whole graph, regression of parameters by a network, gradient descent, gradient free search (Bayesian optimization, simulated annealing, Markov chain Monte Carlo, reinforcement learning), or autoregressive generation of the graph itself. (11 October 2026)
- Refinement: nearly every pipeline ends with a local parameter optimization on a frozen topology, because generation or retrieval alone lands near the target but not on it. (11 October 2026)
- What changes for us: Copernicus is the forward evaluator, the output is one height channel rather than a rendered SVBRDF, and to our knowledge Copernicus exposes no automatic differentiation, so every gradient based variant needs a replica or a proxy outside Houdini (UNVERIFIED that Houdini 22 has no differentiable cooking path). (11 October 2026)

### 1.2 Lefebvre and Poulin 2000, analysis and synthesis of structural textures (11 October 2026)

- Reference: L. Lefebvre, P. Poulin, "Analysis and Synthesis of Structural Textures", Graphics Interface 2000, pp. 77 to 86, https://graphicsinterface.org/wp-content/uploads/gi2000-12.pdf (read in full for the tiling and wood parts). (11 October 2026)
- Problem: non parametric synthesis destroys the semantics of bricks and tiles (mortar lines, tile size, regular layout), so the authors fit a small procedural model for two classes, rectangular tilings and wood. (11 October 2026)
- Graph representation: a fixed tiling model with six scalars (tile width, tile height, pattern orientation, mortar thickness, number of tiles along the wall, relative offset between successive rows) plus exemplar regions that fill the tile and mortar interiors. (11 October 2026)
- Step 1: the user produces a clean binary mask, tiles white and mortar black, with Sobel or pyramidal edge detectors, histogram thresholding and morphological opening, closing and erosion; an experienced user needs a few minutes. (11 October 2026)
- Step 2: the FFT of the mask is taken; the brightest group of points other than DC gives the dominant frequency; in a 2R by 2R spectrum the tile height, expressed as a fraction of the image height, equals R divided by twice the distance from that peak to the center, and the pattern orientation is the arctangent of the peak offset. (11 October 2026)
- Step 3: the mask is rotated back to the image axes; horizontal scanlines count runs of tile pixels bounded by mortar, and the median run is the tile width; vertical scanlines give the mortar thickness in the same way. (11 October 2026)
- Step 4: the relative offset between rows is measured from the centers of a tile and of its neighbor in the adjacent row, repeated over several tiles with outliers discarded; a ratio of offset to width close to zero signals the aligned grid typical of ceramic tiles. (11 October 2026)
- Step 5: tile and mortar interiors are synthesized with the Heeger and Bergen pyramid algorithm from regions the user selects, avoiding highlights and occlusions. (11 October 2026)
- What is optimised: nothing iteratively; every parameter is computed in closed form from one measurement (a spectral peak, a median of run lengths). (11 October 2026)
- Limits: precision depends on the mask; mortar too thin in the photo prevents counting tiles automatically; vertical mortar lines are weak in the spectrum and need the spatial scanlines; roughness and specularity are not extracted; highlights spoil interior synthesis. (11 October 2026)
- Wood (same paper): ten scalars (trunk center, tilt and rotation, growth frequency, early to late wood ratio and colors, turbulence intensity and frequency) estimated from a mask by the same FFT peak, by counting pixels along scanlines, and by column variance; knots are not handled. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable for us: this is the cheapest exact route to the deterministic lattice of a tile height; one FFT peak gives period and angle, run length medians give grout width, tile size and row offset, and those numbers map directly onto `tilepattern` parameters (method M05 in section 5). (11 October 2026)

### 1.3 Bourque and Dudek 2004, procedural texture matching and transformation (11 October 2026)

- Reference: E. Bourque, G. Dudek, "Procedural Texture Matching and Transformation", Computer Graphics Forum 23(3), 2004, pp. 461 to 468, https://diglib.eg.org/handle/10.2312/CGF.v23i3pp461-468 (abstract read). (11 October 2026)
- Principle: a library of procedural shaders is searched; for each input sample the appropriate shader and its parameters are found by sampling the parameter domain under a texture similarity function, and smooth paths in parameter space morph between samples and between shaders. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: the earliest form of "retrieve a recipe, then search its parameters under a texture similarity", which is method M17 in section 5; the similarity function itself is UNVERIFIED (abstract only). (11 October 2026)

### 1.4 Hu, Dorsey and Rushmeier 2019, a novel framework for inverse procedural texture modeling (11 October 2026)

- Reference: Y. Hu, J. Dorsey, H. Rushmeier, ACM Transactions on Graphics 38(6), SIGGRAPH Asia 2019, https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/node/199 (abstract, summary and figure captions read; full text not read). (11 October 2026)
- Step 1: textures are grouped into high level classes; for each class a large collection of real images and of procedural node graph models is assembled. (11 October 2026)
- Step 2: unsupervised learning on the real images of a class discovers sub classes, and the outputs of every procedural model are classified into those sub classes. (11 October 2026)
- Step 3: one convolutional network per procedural model learns to regress the parameters that produced a given output, from synthetic samples of that model. (11 October 2026)
- Step 4: at run time the exemplar is classified into a sub class, the models that produce that sub class are selected, and their networks predict parameters in a single forward pass. (11 October 2026)
- What is optimised: network weights, offline; nothing is optimized per exemplar at use time; the training loss of the regressors is UNVERIFIED (not read). (11 October 2026)
- Reported speed: the fitted procedural textures regenerate at 4K in about 160 ms and at 8K in about 1.5 s. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: only library models can be recovered, and a routing error selects a topology that cannot express the exemplar; regression quality depends on how well synthetic samples cover real images (UNVERIFIED, inferred from the design). (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: amortized inversion; if the same Copernicus tile template is reused on many references, a small regressor trained on thousands of cooked variants predicts its parameters instantly (method M18). (11 October 2026)

### 1.5 Guo, Hašan, Yan and Zhao 2020, Bayesian inference for procedural material parameters (11 October 2026)

- Reference: Y. Guo, M. Hašan, L. Yan, S. Zhao, "A Bayesian Inference Framework for Procedural Material Parameter Estimation", Computer Graphics Forum 39(7), Pacific Graphics 2020, pp. 255 to 266, https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.01067 (abstract read). (11 October 2026)
- Principle: estimation from a photograph is posed as Bayesian inverse rendering; optimization gives point estimates and Markov chain Monte Carlo explores the posterior, so several plausible parameter sets are returned; discrete and continuous parameters are both handled. (11 October 2026)
- Test materials: wall plaster, leather, wood, anisotropic brushed metal and layered metallic paint, on synthetic and real targets. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: MCMC over a frozen COP graph is a valid black box sampler when parameters are discrete (tile counts, seeds, pattern presets), and the spread of the accepted samples shows which parameters the reference does not constrain (variant of method M15); the summary statistics used in their likelihood are UNVERIFIED. (11 October 2026)

### 1.6 MATch, Shi et al. 2020, differentiable material graphs for procedural material capture (11 October 2026)

- Reference: L. Shi, B. Li, M. Hašan, K. Sunkavalli, T. Boubekeur, R. Měch, W. Matusik, ACM Transactions on Graphics 39(6), article 196, SIGGRAPH Asia 2020, https://match.csail.mit.edu/ and https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/134067 (abstract and project page read; full text not read). (11 October 2026)
- Step 1: a production Substance graph with hundreds to thousands of parameters is translated by the DiffMat library into a differentiable node graph assembled from differentiable building blocks. (11 October 2026)
- Step 2: a rendering layer is appended so that the chain from graph parameters to rendered image is differentiable end to end. (11 October 2026)
- Step 3: a deep feature based step selects a suitable graph among many and initializes its parameters, which lets the approach scale to large libraries. (11 October 2026)
- Step 4: gradient based optimization adjusts the parameters until the render matches the target flash photograph under a style transfer loss. (11 October 2026)
- What is optimised: continuous parameters of differentiable nodes; generator nodes (noise and pattern sources) were not optimized in 2020, which is the gap later closed by differentiable proxies (UNVERIFIED from the 2020 text; consistent with Hu et al. 2022, who list MATch generators such as Brick, Stripe, Scratches, Tile and Arc Pavement as non differentiable). (11 October 2026)
- Loss as implemented today in DiffMat (code read): the default metric adds a VGG19 texture descriptor with weight 1.0 and the magnitude of the 2D real FFT with weight 0.001; the descriptor concatenates Gram matrices taken at five depths of VGG19 (max pooling replaced by average pooling), each normalized by the pixel count, at three scales by default (full, half and quarter resolution); optional metrics compare a bicubic 16 by 16 thumbnail in L1, a weighted Lab thumbnail, or LPIPS (https://github.com/mit-gfx/diffmat, files diffmat/optim/metric.py and diffmat/optim/descriptor.py). (11 October 2026)
- Limits: the topology must already be able to express the target, hence graph selection; unsupported nodes are pre cached with Substance tools; high VRAM; square textures only; one material graph per file (DiffMat README). (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: the loss design is the lesson; Gram statistics plus a spectrum term plus a tiny thumbnail term separate "same texture" from "same layout", which is exactly our split between stochastic and deterministic content (section 3). (11 October 2026)

### 1.7 DiffMat v2, Li, Shi and Matusik 2023, proxy free mixed integer optimization (11 October 2026)

- Reference: B. Li, L. Shi, W. Matusik, "End-to-end Procedural Material Capture with Proxy-Free Mixed-Integer Optimization", ACM Transactions on Graphics 42(4), article 153, SIGGRAPH 2023, DOI 10.1145/3592132, code https://github.com/mit-gfx/diffmat (README read; the paper PDF exceeded the fetch limit). (11 October 2026)
- Principle: gradient based optimization of continuous parameters combined with gradient free search of discrete parameters, without trained neural proxies; v2 also implements noise generators natively instead of relying on proprietary tools. (11 October 2026)
- Tools exposed: a gradient optimizer, a hybrid mixed integer optimizer, optional Bayesian optimization (scikit-optimize, Ax) and simulated annealing (simanneal), plus a sampler that perturbs continuous parameters to create variations. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: integer parameters such as rows, columns, splits and seeds go to a discrete search while continuous parameters such as bevel width go to a local continuous search; a COP template needs exactly this split (method M15). (11 October 2026)

### 1.8 Hu et al. 2022, an inverse procedural modeling pipeline for SVBRDF maps (11 October 2026)

- Reference: Y. Hu, C. He, V. Deschaintre, J. Dorsey, H. Rushmeier, ACM Transactions on Graphics 41(2), 2022, arXiv 2109.06395, https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2109.06395 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Inputs: albedo, normal and roughness maps; the normal map is integrated into a height map by Poisson reconstruction because height is easier to proceduralize. (11 October 2026)
- Step 1, decomposition: the material is split recursively into a tree of sub materials; each tree node holds a mask locating its sub material, and leaves hold local appearance. (11 October 2026)
- Step 2, segmentation: spectrum aware KNN matting from a few user scribbles, using albedo, height, roughness, position and a local noise spectrum (Welch estimate reduced to three dimensions by PCA); the alpha mattes are thresholded into binary masks. (11 October 2026)
- Step 3, instances: repeated elements such as tiles are cut out of a mask, rescaled to a common bounding box, described by color histograms and local spectra, and grouped by agglomerative clustering. (11 October 2026)
- Step 4, procedural masks: Point Process Texture Basis Functions are fitted to each binary mask by a nearest neighbor query in a database of about 450 thousand masks (LBP, VGG19 Gram and Fourier power spectrum features reduced to 512 dimensions), then by coordinate descent alternating Powell's method for continuous parameters and Bayesian optimization for discrete ones. (11 October 2026)
- Step 5, noise appearance: each leaf is decomposed by progressive Gaussian filtering with growing kernels; every residual layer is fitted with random phase noise from a sliding window spectrum estimate; the mean of the coarsest layer is the base value; holes are inpainted (Telea, PatchMatch) and a Gabor noise basis approximates the inpainted spectrum; color is synthesized in PCA space and then histogram matched. (11 October 2026)
- Step 6, joint refinement: each noise map passes through an optimizable Gaussian filter (intensity, bias, blur), masks are blurred, layers are multiplied by masks and combined bottom up, rendered with Cook Torrance GGX under random lights, and optimized with L-BFGS-B (learning rate 0.005, about 200 steps). (11 October 2026)
- Loss: a style term (L1 between VGG Gram matrices of renders) plus a weighted SSIM term (L1 between SSIM indices). (11 October 2026)
- Cost: under five minutes of user interaction and about twenty minutes of unoptimized computation. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: dense or overlapping elements (pebbles), semantic contours (a flower outline is lost by the PPTBF mask), strongly directional normals, and dependence on user scribbles because automatic segmentation failed on their materials. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: the closest published analogue of our problem, masks first, then one noise model per region, then a short joint refinement; masks with semantic shapes, such as painted motif lines, must not be approximated by a statistical mask model but traced (method M08). (11 October 2026)

### 1.9 Hu et al. 2022, node graph optimization using differentiable proxies (11 October 2026)

- Reference: Y. Hu, P. Guerrero, M. Hašan, H. Rushmeier, V. Deschaintre, SIGGRAPH 2022 Conference Proceedings, DOI 10.1145/3528233.3530733, arXiv 2207.07684, https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2207.07684 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Problem: generator nodes (Brick, Stripe, Scratches, Tile Paraboloid, Tile Brick, Arc Pavement, and PPTBF) are not differentiable, so earlier pipelines could only tune the filters downstream of them. (11 October 2026)
- Proxy: a deterministic StyleGAN2 variant (noise inputs removed) maps normalized parameters through fully connected layers into AdaIN modulated convolutions; each proxy learns from 300 thousand synthetic 256 by 256 outputs sampled around the parameter values artists used in more than 7000 Substance Source graphs. (11 October 2026)
- Proxy training loss: L1 on pixels plus L1 on VGG19 features (weight 10) plus L1 on Gram matrices, with an optional adversarial term for stochastic generators; Adam at 0.0025, 60 epochs, two to three days on one RTX 3090, each proxy under 100 MB. (11 October 2026)
- Stage I: optimize filter parameters only, generators frozen, with a multi scale Gram loss at 256, 128 and 64 pixels. (11 October 2026)
- Stage II: draw 500 generator initializations, keep the best by a grayscale deep feature L1 loss, then optimize all parameters through the proxies with feature L1 plus 0.05 times Gram L1 at several resolutions (Adam, learning rate 0.02 halved every 200 steps). (11 October 2026)
- Stage III: swap the proxies back for the real generators and fine tune the filter parameters with the stage I loss. (11 October 2026)
- Cost: three to five minutes for about 1000 steps once proxies exist; five to ten hours of data generation and two to three days of training per proxy. (11 October 2026)
- Limits stated: local minima on complex graphs; the loss does not match elements pixel by pixel, so specific details such as wood knots are missed; element counts can be off by one (a 7 by 7 target produced 6 by 7); proxies fail outside the artist parameter range; FX map nodes stay non differentiable. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: the off by one count proves that a statistical loss must not decide integer structure; counts and periods must come from direct measurement (FFT, run lengths) and only the remaining continuous parameters go to optimization. (11 October 2026)

### 1.10 MatFormer, Guerrero et al. 2022, a generative model for procedural materials (11 October 2026)

- Reference: P. Guerrero, M. Hašan, K. Sunkavalli, R. Měch, T. Boubekeur, N. J. Mitra, ACM Transactions on Graphics 41(4), SIGGRAPH 2022, arXiv 2207.01044, https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2207.01044 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Graph representation: operator types as a token sequence with node depth; parameters as a flattened sequence of non default values sorted by name with a companion index sequence, quantized to 32 levels; edges as a sequence of slot indices where each consecutive pair is one edge. (11 October 2026)
- Model: three GPT 2 style transformers (2 layers, 4 heads, 64 dimensional features): a node generator, a parameter generator conditioned on the node type, and an edge generator using a pointer network over slot embeddings. (11 October 2026)
- Validity: during inference invalid choices receive zero probability (edges only from output to input slots, no cycles, parameter values inside their ranges). (11 October 2026)
- Data: about 2.8 thousand Substance Source graphs (filtered from about 4 thousand), augmented to about 280 thousand by perturbing parameters by up to 20 percent. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: unconditional generation only, bounded by training diversity, parameters and edges generated independently, no semantic handles. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: a graph serialized as node, parameter and edge sequences with validity masks becomes searchable by any generator, including a language model (method M19). (11 October 2026)

### 1.11 Hu et al. 2023, generating procedural materials from text or image prompts (11 October 2026)

- Reference: Y. Hu, P. Guerrero, M. Hašan, H. Rushmeier, V. Deschaintre, SIGGRAPH 2023 Conference Proceedings, DOI 10.1145/3588432.3591520, arXiv 2304.13172, https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2304.13172 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Conditioning: a frozen CLIP ViT L/14 embedding of the image prompt (text passes through a prior that maps text embeddings to texture image embeddings) is projected by an MLP and added to each transformer encoder layer. (11 October 2026)
- Representation: node, edge (pointer head) and single parameter sequences; 128 quantization bins instead of 32; the parameter model sees nodes and edges through a six layer GCN. (11 October 2026)
- Data: 4,667 cleaned Substance Source graphs of at most 80 nodes, 100 parameter sets each, 466,700 graphs in total. (11 October 2026)
- Inference: nucleus sampling with top p 0.9 and semantic validity checks, about 1.3 s per graph; the top 5 graphs ranked by CLIP cosine or sliced Wasserstein distance are kept. (11 October 2026)
- Post optimization: only optimizable nodes are refined, topology frozen; the reported style loss is L1 on VGG Gram matrices plus 0.1 times L1 on 16 by 16 thumbnails; about two to three minutes per graph on an RTX 3090. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: scarce artist data, quantization errors, details that do not match the input, text prompts not always realistic, and no topology change during optimization. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: "generate several candidates, rank them by a cheap texture distance, refine the best few" is the right outer loop; ranking should use our stochastic distance while final acceptance uses the deterministic one (section 3). (11 October 2026)

### 1.12 Li et al. 2024, procedural material generation with reinforcement learning (11 October 2026)

- Reference: B. Li, Y. Hu, P. Guerrero, M. Hašan, L. Shi, V. Deschaintre, W. Matusik, ACM Transactions on Graphics 43(6), SIGGRAPH Asia 2024, DOI 10.1145/3687979, https://research.adobe.com/publication/procedural-material-generation-with-reinforcement-learning and https://github.com/adobe-research/ProcMatRL (abstract and README read). (11 October 2026)
- Principle: a procedural material is retrieved for the target; a transformer parameter generator (a conditional MatFormer) is pre trained with supervision on synthetic data, then fine tuned by reinforcement learning (PPO, according to the training script names) with a reward based on appearance similarity, on synthetic and real images; inference ends with a differentiable post optimization. (11 October 2026)
- Why it matters: reinforcement learning removes three obstacles at once, scarce procedural training data, the gap between synthetic and real images, and the need for a differentiable loss. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: any non differentiable composite metric, such as mask IoU or component counts, can serve as a reward; the exact reward used in the paper is UNVERIFIED (not stated in the abstract or README). (11 October 2026)

### 1.13 VLMaterial, Li et al. ICLR 2025, procedural materials from a vision language model (11 October 2026)

- Reference: B. Li, R. Wu, A. Solar-Lezama, C. Zheng, L. Shi, B. Bickel, W. Matusik, ICLR 2025, arXiv 2501.18623, https://arxiv.org/html/2501.18623v2 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Representation: Blender procedural material graphs transpiled into Python programs (one function creating nodes, links and parameters), capped at 30 nodes and 2,048 tokens. (11 October 2026)
- Model: LLaVA NeXT (CLIP ViT L/14 encoder, LLaMA 3 8B decoder) fine tuned with LoRA of rank 8 for five epochs on eight H100 GPUs over three days. (11 October 2026)
- Data: 3,663 free Blender materials curated to about 1.6 thousand; GPT 4o mini produced 50.4 thousand structurally distinct programs by crossing programs over, and parameter augmentation brought the set above 550 thousand materials. (11 October 2026)
- Inference: up to 50 programs are sampled until 20 are valid; the one with the lowest style loss is kept and refined by an MCMC local parameter search (200 iterations, worse samples accepted with probability 0.05), without gradients. (11 October 2026)
- Metrics: style loss, sliced Wasserstein distance, CLIP cosine and program correctness. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: intricate textures exceed 30 Blender nodes; correctness drops beyond about 22 nodes; under a tight sampling budget nearest neighbor retrieval beats generation on real images. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: a program form of the graph lets a language model propose whole recipes, and a gradient free MCMC polish suffices; for us the program is the Python that builds the COP network. (11 October 2026)

### 1.14 MultiMat, Belouadi, Boubekeur and Kaiser, ICLR 2026, multimodal program synthesis (11 October 2026)

- Reference: J. Belouadi, T. Boubekeur, A. Kaiser, "MultiMat: Multimodal Program Synthesis for Procedural Materials using Large Multimodal Models", ICLR 2026, arXiv 2509.22151, https://arxiv.org/html/2509.22151v2 (read). (11 October 2026)
- Principle: text only program synthesis lacks the visual feedback an artist relies on; MultiMat generates one node at a time in topological order, executes it, renders its intermediate output and feeds that image back into the context of a large multimodal model. (11 October 2026)
- Representation: CompactSBS, a YAML text form of Substance graphs transpiled back to SBS for execution; "mixed" conditioning adds an image per node to the text, "graph" conditioning shows one picture of the whole graph with embedded outputs. (11 October 2026)
- Model and data: Qwen2.5 VL 7B fine tuned on more than 6,000 materials from Adobe's Substance 3D Assets repository, excluding graphs above 128 nodes and graphs with embedded bitmaps. (11 October 2026)
- Search: a constrained tree search where a node that fails to transpile or execute is resampled, and repeated failures trigger backtracking that discards 2 to the power (i minus 1) recent nodes at iteration i; validity is the only filter, and for the human study 40 candidates per image were sampled and the best by DreamSim kept. (11 October 2026)
- Results: conditional DreamSim and CLIP scores above VLMaterial; parameter optimization raises perceptual similarity but also raises KID. (11 October 2026)
- Limits: days of training on eight A100 GPUs, OCR like misreading of node names in graph images, failures on intricate patterns. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: "build one node, render it, look, continue or backtrack" is our one layer at a time protocol; what MultiMat lacks is a value function, which the section 3 metric can supply (method M19). (11 October 2026)

