### 1.16 Semi procedural and hybrid approaches (11 October 2026)

- Guehl et al. 2020: P. Guehl, R. Allègre, J. M. Dischler, B. Benes, E. Galin, "Semi-Procedural Textures Using Point Process Texture Basis Functions", Computer Graphics Forum 39(4), EGSR 2020, pp. 159 to 171, https://diglib.eg.org/handle/10.1111/cgf14061 (abstract read). (11 October 2026)
- Guehl principle: structure is procedural and color is data driven; a PPTBF extends sparse convolution noise with rich kernels, a window function combined with a correlated mixture of Gabor functions, covering cells, cracks, grains, scratches, spots, stains and waves; thresholding yields a binary structure. (11 October 2026)
- Guehl parameters: estimated automatically from a binary structure exemplar (procedure UNVERIFIED in detail; Hu et al. 2022 fit PPTBF by a database query followed by Powell and Bayesian coordinate descent, see 1.8); color is placed by a multiscale parallel texture synthesis by numbers constrained by the PPTBF; outputs are parametric, infinite and free of repetition. (11 October 2026)
- Hu et al. EGSR 2022: Y. Hu, M. Hašan, P. Guerrero, H. Rushmeier, V. Deschaintre, "Controlling Material Appearance by Examples", Computer Graphics Forum 41(4), pp. 117 to 128, DOI 10.1111/cgf.14591, https://research.adobe.com/publication/controlling-material-appearance-by-examples (abstract level). (11 October 2026)
- Hu EGSR 2022 principle: a tileable MaterialGAN is trained and its latent space optimized through differentiable rendering so that an existing material keeps its large scale structure while acquiring the micro and meso scale texture of user photos, with region level control over several targets. (11 October 2026)
- Actionable: this hybrid split matches our needs; the deterministic skeleton (grid, bevels, lines) stays procedural and exact, while micro detail may borrow the reference statistics (method M14) where pure noise cannot match them. (11 October 2026)

### 1.17 Image to material tools and monocular geometry estimation (11 October 2026)

- Substance 3D Sampler, Image to Material: two algorithms, "AI Powered" (machine learning that recognizes shapes and objects to produce normal, height and roughness and removes lighting from base color) and "B2M" (non AI, less accurate but tolerant of more inputs); the AI path appeared in Substance Alchemist 2020.2 and was retrained on all material types, with a material type parameter, in Sampler 4.2 (September 2023); large images are slow, so layer resolution must be managed (https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-sampler/using/filters/tools/image-to-material and https://helpx.adobe.com/substance-3d-sampler/release-notes/version-4-2.html). (11 October 2026)
- Materialize: free GPL tool by Bounding Box Software (Mike Voeller) that converts photos into PBR maps, including diffuse to height with slider adjustments and height to normal; its internal height estimator is UNVERIFIED, plausibly a multi scale luminance estimate (https://developer.valvesoftware.com/wiki/Materialize). (11 October 2026)
- ArmorLab: standalone application from the Armory3D authors that extracts base color, height, normal, occlusion and roughness from a photo with a pre trained deep network, creates seamless materials from text prompts, and offers tiling, upscale and inpaint nodes; paid (from 19 USD), preview quality (https://armory3d.org/lab and https://armory.itch.io/armorlab). (11 October 2026)
- ControlMat: G. Vecchio et al., ACM Transactions on Graphics 2024, arXiv 2309.01700; a diffusion model conditioned on one photo under uncontrolled lighting outputs tileable base color, roughness, metallic, height, normal and opacity, with rolled diffusion for tileability and patched diffusion for large outputs, trained on Substance 3D Materials (https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01700). (11 October 2026)
