## 3. Comparison metrics for height maps (11 October 2026)

### 3.1 Why the evaluation must separate deterministic structure from stochastic detail (11 October 2026)

- Model: write the target as T = D_T + S_T and the output as O = D_O + S_O, where D is the deterministic part we intend to place exactly (grid, bevels, grout, traced motif lines, chips at measured positions) and S a zero mean stochastic part (mottling, glaze ripple, micro pits) with the same variance σ² in both images, the two realizations being independent. (11 October 2026)
- Consequence: the expected squared pixel error equals (D_T − D_O)² + 2σ² when the output carries a correct stochastic layer, and (D_T − D_O)² + σ² when it carries none; per pixel MSE and MAE therefore reward deleting correct stochastic detail by exactly σ², the classic regression to the mean trap (elementary identity, stated here as reasoning). (11 October 2026)
- SSIM falls into the same trap: its structure term is a local correlation, zero in expectation between independent realizations, so a smooth output scores better than a statistically perfect noisy one (reasoning from the SSIM definition). (11 October 2026)
- LPIPS is not a way out: the DISTS authors show that PSNR, SSIM and LPIPS all rank a JPEG compressed grass image above a resampled grass image, against human ratings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07728, abstract read). (11 October 2026)
- Floor: with a statistically perfect stochastic layer, the full image RMSE cannot drop below the square root of (RMSE_D² + 2σ²); estimating σ on the reference residual gives this floor before any work starts, and a full image RMSE near it means finished even though it is not zero (PROPOSED use of the identity). (11 October 2026)
- Protocol that follows: pixel identity is demanded only on deterministic content (masks, averaged profiles, coarse thumbnails), and distributional identity on the stochastic residual, measured relative to the reference's own variability. (11 October 2026)

### 3.2 Catalogue of metrics, with robustness to random detail (11 October 2026)

- MAE and RMSE: mean absolute and root mean square differences, expressed in height units or in percent of the reference range between its 1st and 99th percentiles; RMSE weighs large local errors (a missing grout line) more than MAE; exact pixel identity measures, not robust to stochastic detail (3.1); use them on deterministic content and on coarse thumbnails only. (11 October 2026)
- SSIM: Z. Wang, A. C. Bovik, H. R. Sheikh, E. P. Simoncelli, "Image Quality Assessment: From Error Visibility to Structural Similarity", IEEE Transactions on Image Processing 13(4), 2004 (recalled reference); local means, variances and covariance in an 11 by 11 Gaussian window of sigma 1.5, constants C1 = (0.01 L)² and C2 = (0.03 L)² with L the dynamic range; for heights set L to the reference range rather than 1.0, otherwise shallow reliefs are flattened by the constants (PROPOSED); not robust to independent noise. (11 October 2026)
- MS SSIM: Z. Wang, E. P. Simoncelli, A. C. Bovik, "Multiscale Structural Similarity for Image Quality Assessment", Asilomar 2003 (recalled reference); contrast and structure terms on five dyadic scales combined with fixed exponents; tells at which scale structure fails; fine scales remain noise intolerant. (11 October 2026)
- LPIPS: R. Zhang, P. Isola, A. A. Efros, E. Shechtman, O. Wang, "The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Deep Features as a Perceptual Metric", CVPR 2018, https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.03924 (recalled reference); distances between unit normalized deep features weighted by linear layers calibrated on human judgments; tolerant to small distortions, not to resampling; expects RGB in the range minus one to one, so a height must be replicated and rescaled. (11 October 2026)
- DISTS: K. Ding, K. Ma, S. Wang, E. P. Simoncelli, "Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity", IEEE TPAMI 44(5), pp. 2567 to 2581, https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07728 (abstract read); combines a texture term on spatial averages of VGG feature maps with a structure term on feature correlations, tuned on human ratings and on crops of the same texture; explicitly tolerant to texture resampling and fairly insensitive to small translations and dilations; non convex, slow to optimize; the best single learned score for "same texture, other realization". (11 October 2026)
- Gradient statistics and structure tensor: gradients by Sobel or central differences; histogram of magnitudes; histogram of orientations weighted by magnitude (for example 36 bins over 180 degrees); structure tensor J = G_ρ ∗ (∇h ∇hᵀ) with eigenvalues λ1 ≥ λ2, coherence ((λ1 − λ2) / (λ1 + λ2))² and dominant orientation ½ atan2(2 J_xy, J_xx − J_yy) (J. Bigün, G. Granlund, ICCV 1987, recalled reference); magnitude and orientation histograms are robust to stochastic placement while the per pixel tensor field is not; gradients are what shading reveals, so a gradient error is more visible than a height offset of equal size. (11 October 2026)
- Radially averaged power spectrum (RAPS): squared FFT modulus of the mean removed image, after a window or after the periodic plus smooth decomposition (L. Moisan, "Periodic Plus Smooth Image Decomposition", Journal of Mathematical Imaging and Vision 39, 2011, recalled reference) to kill the border cross, averaged over rings of constant radial frequency and compared on a log scale per octave; an angular energy profile per orientation sector adds anisotropy; robust to placement; blind to phase, so a crack network and a noise of the same spectrum look alike. (11 October 2026)
- Histogram distances: the 1D Wasserstein distance W1 equals the mean absolute difference between the sorted values of two images of equal size, in height units; the Kolmogorov Smirnov statistic is the largest CDF gap; robust to placement and directly readable ("at equal rank the output sits 0.4 percent too low"); compute them globally and per class mask (tile face, grout, lines), because one global histogram can match while classes are swapped. (11 October 2026)
- Black and white masks at absolute reference thresholds: thresholds are read once on the reference histogram (valleys between modes, or depths with a physical meaning such as halfway between grout floor and tile face) and applied unchanged to every output; percentiles recomputed on the output are forbidden because a wrong amplitude would still yield a perfect mask; compare by IoU = |A ∩ B| / |A ∪ B| (Jaccard) or Dice, plus a boundary F1 at 1 to 2 pixels of tolerance for thin structures; pixel exact for deterministic classes, while stochastic classes are compared through mask statistics (area fraction, components). (11 October 2026)
- Connected component statistics: label each mask with 4 or 8 connectivity, then compare count, area distribution (median, quartiles, KS distance on log areas), elongation (ratio of principal axes), total skeleton length for cracks and Euler number for networks; robust to placement; the natural metric for chips, pinholes and crack networks; in Copernicus `segmentbyconnectivity` and `statisticsbyid` (per island area, minimum and maximum written as per pixel layers, documentation read) provide the ingredients. (11 October 2026)
- Granulometry: area removed by morphological openings (bright features) or closings (dark features) of growing structuring elements gives a size distribution independent of position (P. Maragos, "Pattern Spectrum and Multiscale Shape Representation", IEEE TPAMI 11(7), 1989, recalled reference); robust to placement; `dilateerode` provides erosion and dilation in Copernicus. (11 October 2026)
- Profile correlation: 1D profiles, either row and column means (grout lines show up as periodic dips) or perpendicular profiles across a grout edge, a bevel or a raised line, aligned and averaged over many sites; compared by Pearson correlation and MAE after aligning periodic profiles by cross correlation; averaging cancels stochastic content, so this is the cleanest pixel level check of a bevel or line cross section. (11 October 2026)
- Patch coverage: bidirectional nearest patch distance, completeness (every reference patch exists in the output) and coherence (every output patch exists in the reference) (D. Simakov, Y. Caspi, E. Shechtman, M. Irani, "Summarizing Visual Data Using Bidirectional Similarity", CVPR 2008, recalled reference); robust to placement; the diagnostic of section 2.1. (11 October 2026)
- Deep feature distributions: Gram or sliced Wasserstein distances on VGG features (2.8); robust to placement, scale dependent, meaningful only against a self baseline (3.3). (11 October 2026)

