### 3.3 Recommended composite evaluation (PROPOSED, numbers to calibrate on real targets) (11 October 2026)

- Step 1, normalization: express every height as a fraction of the reference range r = P99 − P1 measured once on the reference; never rescale the output by its own range, otherwise amplitude errors disappear. (11 October 2026)
- Step 2, structure masks: threshold the reference at absolute levels chosen on its histogram to obtain the deterministic classes (tile face, bevel ring, grout, raised lines, and the chips and cracks we decide to place exactly); apply the same thresholds to the output. (11 October 2026)
- Step 3, stochastic residual: estimate it on both images with the same operator, for example the image minus a Gaussian blur of sigma 2 pixels, inside each class eroded by 4 pixels so that edges do not leak into it; σ of the reference residual gives the RMSE floor of 3.1. (11 October 2026)
- Step 4, self baseline: compute every stochastic metric between two disjoint halves of the reference (left against right, top against bottom, aligned on the tile period); the value B is the reference's own variability, and an output is statistically identical when d(output, reference) stays within a small multiple of B. (11 October 2026)
- Step 5, gates: evaluate the gates of the table below; a layer is finished when its gate passes, and the work is finished when every gate passes or when the full image RMSE is within 10 percent of the floor. (11 October 2026)
- Step 6, single score for ranking candidates: score = mean over gates of max(0, m_g / τ_g − 1), with m_g the measured value and τ_g its threshold, after turning "higher is better" metrics into 1 − value; zero means all gates pass, and lower is better. (11 October 2026)
- Step 7, acceptance of a new layer: keep it only if it improves the metric of the gate it targets by at least 5 percent relative, or makes that gate pass, while no other gate worsens by more than 2 percent relative; otherwise revert it and try the next candidate. (11 October 2026)
- Thresholds below assume a 2048 by 2048 map; pixel tolerances scale with resolution, percentages do not. (11 October 2026)

