## 5. Actionable synthesis: methods for choosing and building the next layer (11 October 2026)

This section turns sections 1 to 4 into 22 methods that differ by principle, not by settings; each gives its principle, its algorithm, the measurements that drive each step, the first four phases on a glazed ceramic wall tile height, its strengths, its failure modes and its cost. Everything in this section is PROPOSED unless a source is cited. (11 October 2026)

### 5.0 Shared notation, measurements and test case (11 October 2026)

- T is the reference height normalized by r = P99 − P1 of the reference; O_k is the cooked output after k layers; R_k = T − O_k is the signed residual. (11 October 2026)
- Absolute thresholds are read once on the reference histogram (or on one fixed transform of it, such as T minus a Gaussian blur of fixed sigma) and reused unchanged for every output, giving class masks M_c(T) and M_c(O_k). (11 October 2026)
- Measurements shared by all methods (section 3): m1 per class histograms and W1; m2 radially averaged spectrum and angular profile; m3 IoU and boundary F1; m4 connected components; m5 averaged cross section profiles; m6 gradient and orientation histograms; m7 DISTS or sliced Wasserstein ratio to the self baseline; m8 per tile statistics by ID; the gates A to J and the acceptance rule of 3.3. (11 October 2026)
- Test case for the "first four phases": a glazed ceramic wall tile height with square tiles, bevelled edges, recessed grout joints, slightly raised painted motif lines, hairline cracks, edge chips and low frequency mottling of the glaze. (11 October 2026)
